<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edicts of Ashoka</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Edicts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> are a series of inscriptions on rocks and pillars issued by the Maurya emperor Ashoka in the third century BCE. They proclaim his commitment to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a>, his remorse after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a> war, and policies of tolerance, welfare and moral government. They are the earliest substantial written records of Indian history and a key source on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a> governance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edicts-of-ashoka.jpg" alt="Edicts of Ashoka" width="296" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sachin kumar tiwary · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashoka_Lauriya_Areraj_inscription.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Edicts are more than thirty inscriptions on pillars, boulders and cave walls, attributed to Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> of the Mauryan Empire. They are found across modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, Bangladesh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, and provide the first tangible evidence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. They are the earliest written and datable texts from India, and since they were cut in stone they survive exactly as inscribed, whereas earlier texts such as the Vedic texts were handed down orally. Ashoka called them Dhaṃma Lipi, inscriptions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a>, and refers to himself as Beloved of the Gods. The identification with Ashoka was confirmed by an inscription found in 1915 at Maski, which uses his name with his titles. James Prinsep deciphered them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The Edicts fall into four groups: Minor Rock Edicts, Minor Pillar Edicts, fourteen Major Rock Edicts with two further ones in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, and seven Major Pillar Edicts. The minor rock edicts are the earliest and are the most religious; they mention the Buddha, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangha">Sangha</a> and Buddhist scriptures. The earliest dated, the Kandahar Bilingual Rock Inscription in Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, was written in the tenth year of his reign, in 260 BCE. The major rock edicts give practical instructions for running the empire, such as irrigation, and set out his belief in peaceful moral conduct, with little personal detail. The Major Pillar Edicts date from his twenty-sixth and twenty-seventh years, and the last is testamental in nature.</p><p>The dhamma they promote is largely ecumenical and non-sectarian. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romila-thapar">Romila Thapar</a> summarises its main principles as non-violence, tolerance of all sects, obedience to parents, respect for brahmins and other teachers, and humane treatment of servants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> reports medical treatment for people and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, better roadside facilities, and officers to oversee the welfare of the people and the spread of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a>. He also records that he sent emissaries and medicinal plants to Hellenistic kings, naming Antiochos and Ptolemy, as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Most inscriptions are in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmi-script">Brahmi script</a>, with Kharoshthi in the northwest and Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> inscriptions in the northwestern areas. The two Indian scripts had become extinct by about the 5th century CE and were undeciphered when the Edicts were investigated in the 19th century. The inscriptions may show Achaemenid influence: lipi, the word used for his writings, is thought to come from an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> word for inscription. They contain the first examples of the Brahmi numerals that were ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>–Arabic numerals. The Chinese traveller Fa Hian described a pillar with an inscription by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>. Christopher I. Beckwith distinguishes Ashoka from the Piyadasi of the major edicts, a view that mainstream scholarship does not accept.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edicts of Ashoka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ashoka's reign added something of its own: the <b>Edicts of Ashoka</b>, inscribed on rocks and pillars, proclaimed dhamma; welfare measures provided wells, rest-houses and medical care for people and animals; and special officers (dhamma-mahamattas) were appointed to promote moral conduct.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>His <b>Rock Edict XIII</b> records 100,000 killed and 150,000 deported and expresses his remorse, a turning point in his commitment to dhamma.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kalinga">Kingdom of Kalinga</a></li></ul></section>
